I think that’s a beautiful—and painful—distinction.

Sometimes you don’t need more fuel. You need someone to help you find your flame again.

People see someone struggling and think they need more resources. More sleep. More money. More discipline. More opportunities. More time.

And those things can help. But they don’t necessarily solve the problem you’re describing.

Because having the capacity to burn isn’t the same as having the desire or spark to ignite.

And maybe the cruelest part is that people keep looking at all your unused potential and asking why you’re not shining.

They see the wax. They see the oil. They remember how brightly you used to burn.

But they don’t understand why you can’t simply light yourself again.

And there’s something particularly human about needing another person’s flame. Sometimes it takes someone who believes in you when you can’t feel that belief yourself. Someone who invites you into an adventure. Someone who makes you laugh unexpectedly. Someone who wants you around not because of what you provide, accomplish, or illuminate, but simply because they enjoy your company.

Not someone demanding that you shine for them.

Someone willing to sit with you in the dark until there’s a little light again.

And there’s another layer to your metaphor I really like:

A candle doesn’t become less of a candle because its flame goes out. Its potential remains. And one candle can light another without losing its own flame.

I don’t experience that exhaustion myself, but I understand the distinction you’re making between being depleted and being unable to ignite.

I want to ask you something, though, because I think it matters.

Do you feel like you’ve lost your spark, or do you feel like you’ve spent so long being everyone else’s light that nobody remembers you need someone to light yours, too?